<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Emishi</h1><p class="st-meta">600 CE – 811 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The peoples of northern Honshu who resisted the Japanese court for two centuries until defeated in the wars of the late 8th and early 9th centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Emishi was the court's term for the inhabitants of the northeast, beyond the frontier of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/yamato">Yamato</a> state, in the regions later called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mutsu-province">Mutsu</a> and Dewa. They were not a single nation but many communities; whether they were related to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ainu">Ainu</a>, or were Japanese speakers who simply lived outside the state, is still debated.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Emishi lived by hunting, fishing, farming and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horse-breeding">horse breeding</a>, and were famed as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mounted-archer">mounted archers</a>. Local chiefs led bands rather than a central government. The court built fortresses such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taga-castle">Taga-jo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isawa-castle">Isawa</a> and settled colonists, while some Emishi accepted court titles and others raided frontier posts.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>During the Thirty-Eight Years' War of 774 to 811, the leader Aterui surrendered to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sakanoue-no-tamuramaro">Sakanoue no Tamuramaro</a> in 802 and was executed. The campaigns ended in 811, and many Emishi were resettled across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, while their northern descendants later supported the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abe-clan-of-mutsu">Abe</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/northern-fujiwara">Northern Fujiwara</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/emishi.jpg" alt="Emishi" width="320" height="244"><figcaption>Image: Unknown artist, 14th century · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emishi_from_an_Emakimono_circa_1324.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>600 CE – 811 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Aterui</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Indigenous beliefs</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ebisu, Ezo (Emishi)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=706&amp;focus=emishi" aria-label="Emishi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8173 881 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Emishi lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8441 1168h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8493 1137h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M8493 1137h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Japan. <a href="/atlas?year=706&amp;focus=emishi">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

